  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 1,
    "lesson_title": "Complex Exponentials as LTI System Inputs",
    "lesson_core_move": "Interpret a complex exponential $e^{j\\omega t}$ as a single-frequency probe input for an LTI system, identifying $\\omega$ as the frequency whose response determines output scaling and phase.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Complex Exponentials as LTI System Inputs",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Complex Exponentials as LTI System Inputs",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "When you analyze an LTI system one frequency at a time, start with the complex exponential input $x(t)=e^{j\\omega t}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=e^{j\\omega t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Here, $\\omega$ is the angular frequency being probed. It is the frequency parameter in the exponent, not the time variable.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{j\\omega t}=\\cos(\\omega t)+j\\sin(\\omega t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Euler's formula shows that this probe is a compact single-frequency representation, not a mixture of frequencies.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Later steps will reuse this same input to read how the LTI system changes the signal's scaling and phase at the same $\\omega$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Complex Exponentials as LTI System Inputs",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Identifying the Angular Frequency in $e^{j\\omega t}$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $x(t)=e^{j7t}$, identify the angular frequency and separate it from the imaginary unit and the time variable.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Compare the signal to the standard probe form $x(t)=e^{j\\omega t}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=e^{j7t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The coefficient multiplying $t$ inside the exponent is the angular frequency, so here $\\omega=7$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega=7",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The $j$ is the imaginary unit and $t$ is the time variable; neither one is the frequency parameter.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Complex Exponentials as LTI System Inputs",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Interpreting $e^{j\\omega t}$ as a Single-Frequency Input",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $x(t)=e^{j\\omega t}$, decide whether the input is single-frequency or multi-frequency and say what makes it a probe signal.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the standard probe form $x(t)=e^{j\\omega t}$. This signal is already written with one angular frequency parameter, $\\omega$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=e^{j\\omega t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because there is only one exponential frequency present, this is a single-frequency complex exponential, not a mixture of frequencies.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That is why it works as a probe input for an LTI system: it isolates one angular frequency at a time.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Complex Exponentials as LTI System Inputs",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Using a Complex Exponential to Probe an LTI System",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "If you want to probe an LTI system at angular frequency $\\omega_0$, what input should you apply and why is that choice useful?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the target frequency $\\omega_0$. Use the matching complex exponential input $x(t)=e^{j\\omega_0 t}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=e^{j\\omega_0 t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This choice is useful because it contains one angular frequency only, so it tests the system one frequency at a time.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The result is a clean probe input: the response at $\\omega_0$ can be examined directly next, without mixing in any other frequencies.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Complex Exponentials as LTI System Inputs",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Connecting Probe Frequency to Output Scaling and Phase",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given a probe input $e^{j\\omega_0 t}$, identify which frequency-dependent system response determines the output's scaling and phase.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the probe frequency $\\omega_0$. The quantity you want is the system response evaluated at that same frequency, written as the frequency response $H(j\\omega_0)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "H(j\\omega_0)=|H(j\\omega_0)|e^{j\\angle H(j\\omega_0)}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The magnitude $|H(j\\omega_0)|$ sets the output scaling, and the angle $\\angle H(j\\omega_0)$ sets the phase shift.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the probe frequency tells you where to look, and $H(j\\omega_0)$ tells you how the system changes that tone without changing the frequency.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
